 ],
 "title": "Programming in Scala (5th edition, Scala 3)",
 "byline": "Martin Odersky, Lex Spoon, Bill Venners and Frank Sommers · Artima, 2021",
 "note": "This book is long and granular, so summaries and terms are given per chapter; each section is still listed with its PDF page range. Conclusion sections, the glossary, bibliography and index are not broken out. Chapters also link to the matching pages of the official Scala 3 documentation at docs.scala-lang.org (separate documentation, not the book’s text). Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "The standard Scala book by the language’s designer, updated for Scala 3. It introduces the language from the REPL and scripts through classes, objects and basic types, builds functional objects (Rational), and covers control structures, functions and closures, control abstraction, composition and inheritance, traits, packages/imports/exports and pattern matching. It then treats lists and other collections, mutable objects with a circuit-simulation case study, the type hierarchy including union and intersection types, type parameterization and variance, enums and ADTs, abstract members and path-dependent types, and Scala 3’s contextual abstractions: givens, extension methods and typeclasses with derivation-style examples, before a deep tour of the collections library and testing.",
 "links_label": "Scala 3 docs"
}